WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.020
A single red thread runs through
five centuries of love poems.

2
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.360
At a Tudor court, a lover
blows the hunting horn,

3
00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.340
then gives up the chase.

4
00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:14.960
Decades later, Shakespeare
insists: "Love’s not Time’s fool,

5
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.430
though rosy lips and cheeks".

6
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.480
Later in the century,
another lover argues:

7
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.920
"Had we but world enough, and
time," The thread runs on,

8
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.800
past ballads and mourning cards, to 1901.

9
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:33.060
So has love changed, or
only how poets write it?

10
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.600
In this mission, you'll watch a speaker
turn death itself into an argument for

11
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.670
desire.

12
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.820
And you'll meet a Victorian poem
that plays with one loaded word,

13
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.760
ruined, and the codes behind it.

14
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.860
Your mission: decide
whether love has changed,

15
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.940
or only how poets write it.
